The error happens because Spring’s **@Transactional** logic uses "proxies." Since Spring needs to wrap your method to start and stop the transaction, the method **cannot be private**. It must be **public** so Spring can override it.
Additionally, to ensure the new transaction (REQUIRES_NEW) actually starts, the method needs to be called in a way that goes through the Spring proxy.
Here is the fixed, full code for the method. I have changed the visibility to **public** to solve your error and kept the success-only counting logic to fix your "105,000 instead of 75,000" bug.
### Updated processChunkRaw Method
```java
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void processChunkRaw(List<JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData> chunk, 
                            String batchId, 
                            String commonRemarks, 
                            String creatorId, 
                            Integer creatorRole, 
                            int globalOffset, 
                            LongAdder globalDebit, 
                            LongAdder globalCredit) {
    
    String sql = "INSERT INTO JOURNAL_REQUEST (REQ_ID, REQ_STATUS, REQ_DATE, CREATOR_ID, CREATOR_ROLE, BATCH_ID, JOURNAL_ID, COMMON_BATCH_REMARKS, PAYLOAD, REQ_BRANCH_CODE, REQ_CURRENCY, REQ_CGL, REQ_AMOUNT, REQ_CSV_DATE, REQ_NARRATION, REQ_PRODUCT) VALUES (JOURNAL_REQUEST_SEQ.nextval, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)";
    Timestamp ts = Timestamp.valueOf(LocalDateTime.now());
    final DateTimeFormatter jsonFmt = DateTimeFormatter.ISO_DATE;
    final BigDecimal SCALE_FACTOR = new BigDecimal("10000");

    try {
        // 1. Execute the batch insert
        jdbcTemplate.batchUpdate(sql, new org.springframework.jdbc.core.BatchPreparedStatementSetter() {
            @Override
            public void setValues(java.sql.PreparedStatement ps, int i) throws java.sql.SQLException {
                JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData r = chunk.get(i);

                int globalIdx = globalOffset + i + 1;
                String jId = batchId + "-" + globalIdx;

                BigDecimal absAmt = (r.amount != null) ? r.amount.abs() : BigDecimal.ZERO;
                boolean isCredit = "Credit".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType) || "Cr".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType);
                BigDecimal signedAmt = isCredit ? absAmt.negate() : absAmt;

                LocalDate rDate = LocalDate.now();
                if (r.isSystemFormat && r.sysDate != null && r.sysDate.length() == 8) {
                    try {
                        rDate = LocalDate.parse(r.sysDate, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("ddMMyyyy"));
                    } catch (DateTimeParseException ignored) {}
                }

                ps.setString(1, "P");
                ps.setTimestamp(2, ts);
                ps.setString(3, creatorId);
                ps.setInt(4, creatorRole != null ? creatorRole : 0);
                ps.setString(5, batchId);
                ps.setString(6, jId);
                ps.setString(7, commonRemarks);
                ps.setString(8, buildJsonPayloadFast(r, signedAmt, rDate, batchId, jId, commonRemarks, globalIdx, jsonFmt));
                ps.setString(9, r.branch);
                ps.setString(10, r.currency);
                ps.setString(11, r.cgl);
                ps.setBigDecimal(12, signedAmt);
                ps.setDate(13, java.sql.Date.valueOf(rDate));
                ps.setString(14, r.remarks);
                ps.setString(15, r.productCode);
            }

            @Override
            public int getBatchSize() {
                return chunk.size();
            }
        });

        // 2. ONLY update global totals if the database insert above was successful.
        // This stops the "Wrong Count" bug because failed/retried chunks won't be counted twice.
        for (JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData r : chunk) {
            BigDecimal absAmt = (r.amount != null) ? r.amount.abs() : BigDecimal.ZERO;
            long scaledValue = absAmt.multiply(SCALE_FACTOR).longValue();

            if ("Credit".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType) || "Cr".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType)) {
                globalCredit.add(scaledValue);
            } else {
                globalDebit.add(scaledValue);
            }
        }

    } catch (org.springframework.dao.DataAccessException e) {
        log.error("JDBC_BATCH_FAIL | Batch: {} | Offset: {} | Error: {}", batchId, globalOffset, e.getMessage());
        throw new CustomExceptions.DatabaseOperationException("Database integrity error during chunk insert", e);
    }
}

```
### Important Note for 500,000 rows:
Since you are calling processChunkRaw from within the same service class (JournalRequestServiceImpl), Spring's transaction proxy will be bypassed if you call it as this.processChunkRaw(...).
To make @Transactional work correctly and handle 500,000 records without locking the whole database, you should either:
 1. **Self-Inject the Service**: Add @Autowired private JournalRequestServiceImpl self; and call it as self.processChunkRaw(...).
 2. **Move the method**: Put this method in a separate @Service (e.g., JournalRowWriterService) and call it from there.
This ensures each 5,000-row chunk has its own separate transaction, which is required for high-volume uploads.
